On Thu, 18 Apr 2002, Russell Coker wrote:

> I have attached my latest patch which just adds a "-s" switch for taking file
> names from standard input and a "-m" switch to not display the missed regular
> expressions afterwards.
>
> Actually maybe it should be the other way around, use a "-m" if you want to
> see the unmatched regex's - they don't seem very useful to me.

I've merged this support, but with a number of modifications.  See the
attached complete setfiles.c and setfiles.8 files.  Explanations
and descriptions of my changes below:

1) The processing for the standard input option (-s) doesn't work well
when run with the reset option (-R) or when run on a non-SELinux kernel.
In those cases, setfiles needs the pathname of the root directory of the
filesystem in order to directly access the persistent label mappings.
The original patch would end up dereferencing nulls if no pathname
argument was specified in those cases, and if you did specify a pathname
argument, there was no guarantee that it had any real relationship to the
files in the input list.  Unless you want to reconstruct the root
directory pathname for the filesystem for each file in the list and use
it, this just won't work.  Hence, I've added explicit tests to ensure that
people do not try to use -s with pathname arguments, the -R option, or on
a non-SELinux kernel, and removed the extraneous code from the standard
input processing.

2) When using the -s option, the existing hash table of associations
between inodes and specifications can't be used safely because it only
maintains the inode number, not the device.  This is fine for the normal
case, because the table is constructed for each filesystem and is then
reset for the next filesystem, so inode numbers are sufficient to uniquely
identify entries.  But there is no guarantee that the input list of file
names will be within a single filesystem.  The associations are only
necessary if we want to detect conflicts for multiple hard links.  For the
moment, I've simply modified setfiles to not maintain or use the
associations when running with -s.  If we want this functionality with -s,
then we'll need to extend the hash table to also use the device number (or
force the input list to be within a single filesystem).

3) The default is now "no warnings", as you suggested.  If you want
warnings, use the new -W option.  The -m option is currently accepted
by getopt (but does nothing now) for compatibility, but I can remove it
if/when you update your packages.

4) I made minor revisions to the man page.

